  "id": "Compound::DB01587 - Gene::2564",
  "verbalization": "Ketazolam is likely to interact with Gabre through a mechanism involving the Gaba receptor system. The evidence suggests that Ketazolam binds to Gabrd, a subunit of the Gaba receptor, and that this binding leads to participation in Gaba receptor activity. This activity is then associated with Gabre, indicating a potential role in the regulation of Gaba receptor function. The consistent binding to Gabrd across multiple paths supports a direct interaction, while the involvement of Gabrb1 in other pathways highlights the possibility of receptor subunit diversity. The integration of these findings suggests that Ketazolam may modulate Gaba receptor activity, potentially influencing neuronal signaling and function. The relationship between Ketazolam and Gabre is thus supported by a series of interconnected molecular interactions, emphasizing the role of Gaba receptors in the pharmacological effects of Ketazolam.",
